		why: "PROMISE, in the export's own inline docblock (handlers/context-assembly-policy.ts:36-38), citing the corpus: \"⭑ THE FULL FIELD SET, for the reason `RecordModelExchange` records: `PER-2` requires that 'authoritative history remains reconstructable' and that current state is 'a cache of this history, not a second authority.' A partial payload would invert that.\" WHO RELIES ON IT: registry.ts:224 routes CreateContextAssemblyPolicy to this handler; seed-workbench.ts:255-268 dispatches that command through `sender(handle, 'seedctx')`, reached from openWorkbench via seedWorkbench→seedPolicyLibrary, so it executes on every demo engine open — and anything reconstructing a policy from the authoritative event stream depends on the emitted payload carrying permittedSources. WHAT THE MUTATION DOES TO THEM: emitting `permittedSources: []` while still writing the full set into the state row makes the state row the sole authority and leaves the event unable to rebuild it — the exact PER-2 inversion the docblock names. It is deliberately chosen to stay CONFORMANT so it does not trip a second conjunct: ContextAssemblyPolicyCreatedPayloadSchema is a strictObject whose array accepts [], so the command still returns ACCEPTED and the event is still emitted; only the reconstructability claim breaks.\n\nOBSERVED: clean: Test Files 1 passed (1) / Tests 3 passed (3)  ->  mutated: Test Files 1 failed (1) / Tests 1 failed | 2 passed (3). Reddened exactly one test, 'creates the policy and EMITS its event with the full field set' at context-assembly-policy.test.ts:63 — AssertionError on toEqual, Received permittedSources: [] vs Expected [{ source: 'RENDERED_INSTRUCTION', scope: 'THIS_TRY' }]. The identity test (:69-81) and the closed-union VALIDATION_FAILED test (:83-95) both stayed GREEN, and `r.status` was still ACCEPTED, so the mutant moved only the payload-completeness conjunct. Restore by inverting my own edit; sha256 back to 856d8c98…e66e, 2382 bytes, byte-identical.",
