		find: "\tconst unsupplied = [...declared].filter((m) => context[m] === undefined);",
		replace: "\tconst unsupplied = [...declared].filter((m) => !context[m]);",
		expectRed: ["packages/rph-application/src/prompt-render.test.ts"],
		why: "The sponsor ruling in the module header (prompt-render.ts:8-12): \"A MISSING PLACEHOLDER REFUSES. SPONSOR RULING, 2026-09-04… The alternative — render the hole and disclose it afterwards — was put to the sponsor and declined… A rendered prompt with a hole in it has already gone to the model by the time anything discloses; the disclosure arrives after the harm. Refusing is the only order that works.\" The refusal is narrow BY DESIGN, and the test that pins the narrowness states the distinction: \"An empty string is a value someone supplied; `undefined` is an absence.\" WHO RELIES: `prepareModelInvocation` (execution-invocation.ts:205) renders every MODEL_INVOCATION step's instruction through this function, so the width of this predicate is the width of the refusal for every model step. WHAT THE MUTATION DOES TO THEM: a truthiness test conflates a supplied empty value with an absent one, so a legitimately empty declared section — the docblock's own example, \"prior findings: (none)\" — is refused. The sponsor's narrow ruling silently becomes a blanket one, and the step that should have run stops instead.\n\nOBSERVED: Anchor measured at line 66; `renderPromptTemplate` declaration spans lines 46-80. CLEAN: Tests 7 passed (7). MUTATED: Tests 1 failed | 6 passed (7); the single red is `CONTROL — the guard is about MISSING values, not about empty ones` with `AssertionError: a supplied empty value is not a missing one: expected false to be true` at prompt-render.test.ts:63. Note the three refusal tests stayed green, as expected — the kill lands on the CONTROL, i.e. on the PRECISION of the refusal rather than on its existence. RESTORED by inverting the edit; sha256 back to the baseline d31a90a0…3dd52.",
