		why: "The FROM_STATES arm of evaluatePrecondition is the single place in the system that mints a source-state refusal's CODE — there is no second evaluator. The ratified enforcement register names this exact arm as the MECHANISM for RPH-INT-003 (packages/rph-domain/src/enforcement-register.ts:1550, `enforcedAt`), and that statement's own `refusalCode` field names the code as RPH_ILLEGAL_STATE_TRANSITION. Every state-advancing command in the engine reaches the professional through this descriptor, and RPH-INT-003 itself cites it as evidence. Mutating the minted code to RPH_VALIDATION_SEMANTIC_FAILED reclassifies an illegal state arrow as a semantic inapplicability: the ratified statement's named code becomes false, and any consumer branching on code loses the distinction between 'you cannot do that from this state' and 'this command does not apply to this target at all'.\n\nOBSERVED: Anchor measured at line 133; evaluatePrecondition's declaration span is 123-150, so attribution lands on this export. Victim clean: `bunx vitest run packages/rph-application/src/handlers/command-precondition.test.ts` = 10 passed (10). Mutated: 1 failed | 9 passed (10) — 'fromStates — the state-set special case > refuses a state outside the set with RPH_ILLEGAL_STATE_TRANSITION and DWP-00's message shape', command-precondition.test.ts:62, AssertionError: expected 'RPH_VALIDATION_SEMANTIC_FAILED' to be 'RPH_ILLEGAL_STATE_TRANSITION'. Reproduced on a second independent apply/run/restore cycle with the identical 1-failed/9-passed signature. Restored by inverting my own edit (exact-once check on the mutant text); sha256 back to 26cbaaab6b25807b2e9f1f4625b3deabbdabf7538a309ae918d95536c4ba7a83 both times.",
