		why: "predicate's own docblock, lines 51-59, declares `errorCode` as the 'Refusal code; defaults to RPH_VALIDATION_SEMANTIC_FAILED (the state arrow is legal — the command is semantically inapplicable to this target).' The caller-declared half is the one with production consequence: pwa-authoring.ts:862-868 passes 'RPH_INVARIANT_VIOLATION' so that a re-remove of an already-REMOVED PWU type reads as an invariant violation rather than a semantic inapplicability, and dwp08-precondition-coverage.test.ts asserts precisely that code end-to-end through the Engine. Eleven production call sites construct predicates this way (assurance.ts:783/1034/1442, governance.ts:389/406/660/719, pwa-authoring.ts:60/862, pwu.ts:99, plus noOpEditPrecondition itself). Dropping the spread silently collapses every caller-declared code to the default, so the handler's deliberate classification of its own refusal is discarded without any call site changing.\n\nOBSERVED: Anchor measured at line 76; predicate's declaration span is 72-76. Victim clean: 10 passed (10). Mutated: 1 failed | 9 passed (10) — 'predicate — the general case > honours a custom errorCode', command-precondition.test.ts:82, AssertionError: expected 'RPH_VALIDATION_SEMANTIC_FAILED' to be 'RPH_INVARIANT_VIOLATION'. tsc --noEmit on packages/rph-application under the mutant emitted nothing — the now-unused `errorCode` parameter is not an error because packages/typescript-config/base.json sets `strict` but neither noUnusedParameters nor noUnusedLocals (verified by grep), so this is not a NO_COMPILE. Reproduced on a second apply/run/restore cycle, identical signature; restored byte-identical both times.",
