		expectRed: ["packages/rph-application/src/handlers/recomposition.test.ts"],
		why: "The refusal message states the operative conjunct in the export's own words: \"an acceptance requires an EFFECTIVE APPROVAL Decision naming THIS CONTRACT as a subject. The acceptance is the Decision; this command only records its effect.\" The docblock is emphatic that the command does not perform the acceptance — it RECORDS the effect of an already-EFFECTIVE Decision, the engine's idiom for applying a Decision across aggregates. What relies on it is the authority boundary REG-D-044 settled after REG-F-085 blocked for twelve days: JPWB-DOC-001 reserves acceptance to Governance through the ratified `decide` verb, and PWU RECOMPOSING -> RECOMPOSED is guarded on \"Recomposition contract satisfied\", so this arrow is the only route to SATISFIED. The mutation leaves every other limb intact (DECISION type, APPROVAL, EFFECTIVE) and changes only WHICH object the Decision must name — from the contract to its parent work unit. Under it, authority granted over one object silently settles a different one: an approval of the parent would be spent to accept the recomposition contract nobody approved, and the completion gate would open on a decision that never addressed it.\n\nOBSERVED: Clean: 15 passed (15). Mutated: 4 failed | 11 passed (15). The discriminating failure is 'refuses a decision that names ANOTHER object — authority does not bleed' at recomposition.test.ts:437, where `expect(r.status).toBe('REJECTED')` gets 'ACCEPTED' — the arrangement approves a Decision naming the PARENT, and under the mutation that decision is wrongly read as accepting the CONTRACT. The happy path (:423) and the two other AcceptRecomposition refusals also redden. Restored by inverting the edit; file sha256 back to 594b98bb…e60ea, `git status` clean.",
