		expectRed: ["packages/rph-application/src/handlers/decomposition-blocking-observations.test.ts"],
		why: "The field's own docblock states the promise: `DecompositionRejected.blockingObservationIds` is \"The BLOCKING SUBSET of the observations the validator cited … DERIVED, NOT ASSERTED: `severity` is read off each cited ASSURANCE_OBSERVATION object, so the caller cannot declare a non-blocking observation to be the thing that blocked … copying `observationIds` wholesale would assert every considered observation was blocking.\" Who relies on it: every reader of the append-only event stream that reconstructs WHY a decomposition was rejected — the rejection event is the record of the blocking basis, and a validator citing a MATERIAL observation alongside a BLOCKING one has considered it, not been blocked by it. The mutation copies the caller's citation wholesale, restoring exactly that defect while leaving the module-level derivation function intact, so the credit attaches to the call site inside validateDecomposition where the promise to production lives. It is not caught by the emitted-event guard — `DecompositionRejectedPayloadSchema.blockingObservationIds` is `z.array(z.string())`, so a wholesale copy still conforms — and it also stops filtering ids that resolve to no ASSURANCE_OBSERVATION at all, so a ghost id would be recorded as a blocking basis. The caller would thereby gain the power to assert, unchallenged and permanently, that any observation it named was the thing that blocked.\n\nOBSERVED: Clean: 4 passed (4). Mutated: 2 failed | 2 passed (4). Killing assertion 'keeps only the BLOCKING-severity observation, not every one the validator cited' — \"MATERIAL is a real severity and is NOT blocking — copying observationIds wholesale would assert that every considered observation was the thing that blocked: expected [ …(2) ] to deeply equal [ 'obs_01ARZ3NDEKTSV4RRFFQ69JC600' ]\". The ghost-id case 'drops an id that resolves to no ASSURANCE_OBSERVATION' reddens with it. Restored by inverting the edit; byte-identical.",
